I'm a parlous una product manager for  kubernetes and Google container engine  there has been a dramatic shift in the  last couple of years in the ability for  enterprises to realize the speed  efficiency and scale benefits of  distributed computing whether it is in  the form of a private cloud on-premise  or public clouds at the scale of Google  container packaged apps are legitimately  portable and fast to deploy because the  packaging format provides independence  from the underlying host file system and  OS distribution so you can deploy an app  quickly and take it anywhere but  containers are only part of the solution  running applications in a sea of  distributed resources poses new  challenges for example how do you  schedule containers to utilize the  underlying resources efficiently when  those resources are unreliable how do  you automatically monitor and recover  containers and applications when they  fail how do you add and remove  containers to scale the application with  demand and with everything moving around  how do clients discover containerized  applications google open sourced  kubernetes to provide a solution for  these and other distributed computing  challenges based on over 12 years of  experience internally out-of-the-box  kubernetes automates container  scheduling health checking and recovery  replication to ensure uptime and  internal network management to ensure  service naming discovery and load  balancing the basic concepts kubernetes  uses to manage container clusters  include a pod which is a group of  containers that are always scheduled  together and have a shared networking  and storage identity pods typically  bundle an app with its helper functions  for example a web server and a file  puller a controller which is a feedback  loop that drives currents  eight towards the desired state this is  the basis for automation at many levels  in kubernetes for example replication  controllers ensure service continuity by  making sure a sufficient number of pods  are running at all times controllers are  also used for auto scaling a service is  an abstraction across pods that together  represent an application a service has a  stable IP address for the external world  thereby hiding the complexity of  dynamically scheduled pods across a  cluster services enable application  discovery networking and load balancing  lastly labels are user-defined metadata  that can be attached to any object in  kubernetes selectors can then be used to  query the object state and define  policies this enables many different  types of management operations for  example automated canary deployments or  rolling updates to name just two our  goal with kubernetes is to meet  customers where they are containers help  you deploy to any cloud kubernetes gives  you a management stack that can go  anywhere containers can the freedom to  deploy on premise in hybrid or multi  cloud environments left customers choose  based on the merits of the underlying  cloud Google container engine provides  the performance scale and efficiency of  the Google cloud platform to kubernetes  customers it is one of the easiest  fastest and most cost-effective ways to  manage containers in production today  this is why thousands of customers run  their production environments with  Google container engine now I would like  to invite one of those customers Tyler  Davis from port comm to talk about how  Google container engine has impacted  their business  I'm Tyler Davis DevOps engineer with  porch comm porches collected a massive  amount of data to provide transparency  to the home improvement industry on who  is the best professional to hire with  terabytes of data from both private and  public sources porch is able to connect  homeowners to the highest quality  professionals through the porch platform  the porch platform is present in over  1700 Lowe stores an available via the  porch retail solution on websites like  wayfair.com  and a CD stores calm for porch our  engineering objectives start with  stability and reliability website uptime  and reliability are paramount no website  needs no customers which means no  revenue similarly developer time is at a  premium kubernetes ease of deployment  accelerates everything kubernetes  reduces the time and overhead for  developers during new service  bootstrapping rolling out bug fixes and  new features and reduces maintenance  time with kubernetes we fail fast but we  fix it faster before December 2014 porch  had a monolithic service architecture as  this crude diagram shows we provision to  bear virtual machines running specific  applications often with less than 10%  utilization due to over provisioning for  unexpected demand also there were load  balancers for each application creating  multiple layers of load balancers  confusing compensating of network of  code problems deployments could take one  to two hours on average sometimes half a  day are longer with inconsistent state  for the duration of the rollout this was  an inefficient and ultimately expensive  implementation under our new  architecture client connections route  through a single Google load balancer  directly into the kubernetes cluster  there is only one load balancer instead  of up to four due to kubernetes ability  to proxy traffic between services  internally it's actually helped us  discover bugs and our own applications  which were masked by the layers of load  balancers before we currently run over a  hundred different services in the  cluster these applications are mostly  stateless synchronizing between other  services and storing State on databases  or memcache outside of the cluster the  large multi terabyte databases  configuration management systems like  chef and other strictly stateful systems  exist outside the cluster on compute  engine VMs Redis and other stateful  services are being migrated into the  cluster as we get more coffee  gke features as a result ports enjoys  the following architecture benefits  developer independence via a  self-service architecture allows  developers to go for commit to deployed  in production in less than 10 minutes  without DevOps involvement deployments  are reliable and can be rolled out or  rolled back in three minutes or less  due to the independent promotion  architecture we can and do deploy as  many as 30 to 50 times a day or more  with almost no risk and no downtime  finally kubernetes allows us to sleep  through events that would otherwise  require a 2 a.m. conference called an  engineering having work with kubernetes  for over a year in production we've  learned a few things clearly deployment  containers which only include the  minimum necessary artifacts and runtime  this will lead to faster deployments and  easier reasoning about deployment or  application failures also don't expect  kubernetes to be anything like what has  come before to your applications like  cattle designed for failures and  redundancy in the app and leverage all  the coolest features available such as  service discovery deployments and health  checks when in doubt experiment thank  you for joining us I hope these tips can  help you to quickly and reliably scale  your own architecture to learn more  visit kubernetes do
 

